\pard\li0\sb0\sa100 {\fs2 }\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa40\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab A number is listed once, even when several zero-byte files have it ({\f1\fs20 9123456.pdf} and {\f1\fs20 09123456.pdf}).}\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa40\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab A number is {\b not} listed when another file of the same number in the folder has content ({\f1\fs20 06111222.pdf} is empty but {\f1\fs20 US6111222B1.pdf} is the real copy). The result names those files.}\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa40\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab Zero-byte files whose names are not US numbers ({\f1\fs20 notes.txt}, {\f1\fs20 WO2010123456.pdf}) are left out and named in the result.}\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa120\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab If no patent copy is zero bytes, nothing is written.}\par
\pard\li0\sb240\sa120\keepn\outlinelevel1 {\b\fs29 The USPTO search}\par
\pard\li0\sb0\sa120 {Everything below was tested on the live Patent Public Search site on 13 October 2026.}\par
\pard\li0\sb0\sa120 {{\b Number spellings.} A {\f1\fs20 .pn.} search finds a document only when the number is spelled exactly the way the site stores it, and that is what the program writes:}\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa40\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab patents below 10,000,000: seven digits with leading zeros ({\f1\fs20 "9123456"}, {\f1\fs20 "0012345"}, {\f1\fs20 "0000001"});}\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa40\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab patents from 10,000,000 on: all eight digits ({\f1\fs20 "10123456"}, {\f1\fs20 "12700000"});}\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa40\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab published applications: all 11 digits ({\f1\fs20 "20120001234"}); this finds A1, A2, A9 and P1 publications alike;}\par
\pard\li360\sb0\sa120\fi-360\tx360 {\bullet\tab letter prefixes: seven characters with the letters counting towards the seven ({\f1\fs20 "D512345"}, {\f1\fs20 "D000126"}, {\f1\fs20 "PP12345"}, {\f1\fs20 "RE45678"}, {\f1\fs20 "H001234"}, {\f1\fs20 "T876006"}, {\f1\fs20 "X000001"}, {\f1\fs20 "AI00100"}), and longer numbers in full ({\f1\fs20 "D1012345"}).}\par
\pard\li0\sb0\sa120 {Any other spelling ({\f1\fs20 "09123456"}, {\f1\fs20 "12345"}, {\f1\fs20 "2012001234"}, {\f1\fs20 "2012/0001234"}, {\f1\fs20 "D0512345"}, {\f1\fs20 "US9123456"}, {\f1\fs20 "9123456B2"}) finds nothing; it never finds a wrong document. A number the site does not have (such as 12,345,678, or the many X numbers missing from it) is simply not found; the other numbers in the search are still found. RD documents are not on the site at all.}\par
\pard\li0\sb0\sa120 {The search ends in {\f1\fs20 .pn.} with the final period. Without it the site searches every field and returns the patents that cite these numbers as well.}\par
\pard\li0\sb0\sa120 {{\b Long lists.} The site refuses a search whose request is 8 KB or more: 131 published applications, 155 patents of 8 digits or 166 older patents in one line. It then shows {\i Unable to process your request, please try again later} (sometimes after an "N results found" count) and lists nothing; trying again does not help. So the program keeps a little below that and puts at most 120 published applications, 143 patents of 8 digits or 153 older patents (or D, RE, PP, H numbers) in one line. When there are more it writes several lines, each a complete search in the same format. Paste and search {\b one line at a time}: lines pasted together run as one search, which is too long.}\par
\pard\li0\sb0\sa120 {{\b Reading the results.} The results are listed newest first, not in the order of the search. Old patents (before 1976) are listed twice, from the USPAT and USOCR databases, so the "results found" count can be higher than the number of documents. A published application and the patent granted on it are one family: the second shows only as a {\b +1} button on the first one's row.}\par
\pard\li0\sb240\sa120\keepn\outlinelevel1 {\b\fs29 Tests}\par
\pard\li0\sb0\sa120 {{\f1\fs20 python3 test_getmssgpatcopies.py} checks the file-name rules, the sort order, the two file formats, the splitting of long USPTO searches, a run over a sample folder and the command line.}\par
